From b7e70e07eb627380a07760e0723449340ea6af11 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 16:53:06 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [AGS] ali3dsw: window-surface present-step rewrite, native
 8/16-bit depth

The last remaining COMPILES blocker: ali3dsw.cpp/.h's SDL_CreateRenderer/
SDL_Texture present step hard-required a 32-bit display
(IsModeSupported() rejected anything but 32, GetDisplayDepthForNativeDepth()
returned a hardcoded 32, CreateVirtualScreen() streamed into an ARGB8888
texture) -- three full-frame 4-byte-per-pixel passes for an 8-bit game,
and 320x200x32 is not a universally available VESA mode on 486-era
cards. Per the 2026-09-10 plan review (PLAN.md, PATCH-SEQUENCE.md's 0029
row), the DOS driver instead presents via SDL_GetWindowSurface +
SDL_BlitSurfaceScaled + SDL_UpdateWindowSurface, at the game's own native
depth (8 or 16, IsModeSupported()/GetDisplayDepthForNativeDepth() updated
to match). With native depth threaded straight through, there is no
conversion step left at all: the virtual screen's own pixel buffer
(Bitmap::GetDataForWriting()/GetPitch()) is wrapped directly as an
SDL_Surface (_vscreenSurface, INDEX8 or RGB565) and blitted onto the
window surface -- no intermediate texture, no Allegro-blit depth
conversion. Real palette sync for the 8-bit path is deferred to the next
patch (this one only attaches a placeholder palette so the surface is
valid); the two gfxfilter_*_sdl_renderer filter classes are deferred to
the patch after that (they still reference SDL_render.h and keep this
file's own build blocked on that header until they're rewritten too --
confirmed by direct isolated compile of this file with a scratch stub
swapped in for them, not landed).

Resolves the SDL_HINT_DOS_MAX_BPP sequencing gap patch 0015 found and
left open: the hint must be set before SDL_InitSubSystem(SDL_INIT_VIDEO),
but sys_main_init() (where VIDEO first initializes) runs from
engine_init_backend(), before any game package loads, so the real native
depth isn't known until here -- the first SetDisplayMode() call, long
after game load. Fixed by re-initializing the video subsystem once,
right before creating this backend's one window: quit SDL_INIT_VIDEO,
set the now-known-depth hint, re-init SDL_INIT_VIDEO so
DOSVESA_VideoInit() (vendor/SDL/src/video/dos/SDL_dosmodes.c) re-enumerates
against it. Confirmed safe by reading DOSVESA_VideoInit()/VideoQuit()
directly: no static across-reinit guard, and this runs before any
window or video-backed input state exists yet.

Traced GetDisplayDepthForNativeDepth()'s two callers (graphics_mode.cpp,
per PATCH-SEQUENCE.md's own open item) for 32-bit assumptions: found
none needing a fix. graphics_mode.cpp treats color_depth generically
throughout; engine_setup.cpp's engine_setup_color_conversions() already
sets Allegro's 16-bit RGB565 shifts (_rgb_r/g/b_shift_16 = 11/5/0)
unconditionally, regardless of display depth, on every AGS platform --
not a DOS-specific gap to fix, and the reason _vscreenSurface always
uses SDL_PIXELFORMAT_RGB565 for a 16-bit virtual screen rather than
querying the window surface's own reported format up front (a real
format mismatch on hardware without exact 565, still open per
PATCH-SEQUENCE.md, is handled generically by SDL_BlitSurfaceScaled's own
format conversion rather than hand-rolled here).

Also, in the course of this rewrite:
- SDL3 removed the window gamma ramp API entirely (no
  SDL_GetWindowGammaRamp/SDL_SetWindowGammaRamp, confirmed by reading
  vendor/SDL/include/SDL3/ directly) -- SupportsGammaControl()/SetGamma()
  become permanent no-ops, not a DOS-specific choice.
- No vsync-aware present call exists for the window-surface path either
  (SDL_UpdateWindowSurface has no vsync concept) -- DoesSupportVsyncToggle()/
  SetVsyncImpl() likewise become permanent no-ops.
- Screen-shake/mirror-display (play.shake_screen_yoff/play.screen_flipped,
  Engine/ac/draw.cpp's render_to_screen(), a real caller of the 3-arg
  Render() overload with non-default flip) still needs honoring: the live
  _vscreenSurface can't be flipped in place (RequiresFullRedrawEachFrame()
  is false, so unredrawn regions may persist across frames), so a lazily
  allocated scratch surface is flipped instead, only when requested.
- The old SDL_RenderClear() every frame kept any letterbox/pillarbox
  border outside the scaled dest rect clean; a plain blit only touches
  its own dest rect, so this rewrite adds an explicit SDL_FillSurfaceRect
  black-fill of the window surface, but only when dest doesn't already
  cover the whole surface (no per-frame cost in the common no-border
  case) -- a real gap this rewrite would otherwise have introduced, not
  present in the original code.
- Dropped now-dead code with no equivalent in the window-surface path:
  the DirectX-exclusive-fullscreen-display workaround (_isDirectX/
  _fullscreenDisplay, meaningless without a real SDL_Renderer to detect),
  the already-unused fix_alpha_blender (confirmed via a tree-wide grep,
  not just this file), and the 24-bit-pretends-to-be-32-bit
  GetSupportedModeList() fallback (BOOTSTRAP-0 probe 3 already confirmed
  SDL_HINT_DOS_MAX_BPP yields an exact 8bpp/16bpp mode list, no
  depth-substitution needed at 8/16).

Verified: ali3dsw.cpp.obj compiles clean in isolation (gmake -f
Engine/CMakeFiles/engine.dir/build.make .../ali3dsw.cpp.obj) against the
real DJGPP build flags, with gfxfilter_sdl_renderer.h/gfxfilter_aa_sdl_renderer.h
temporarily swapped for a minimal SDL3-renamed scratch version (not
landed -- the real fix is the next patch) to isolate this file's own
correctness from that still-open blocker. Full build/link (COMPILES)
confirmed once the next two patches (palette sync, filter collapse)
land in the same session.
